er points in cluster of (lambda_death - lambda_birth). The lambda_birth is when the point becomes part of that cluster? Actually, HDBSCAN uses \"lambda birth\" and \"lambda death\" per point in the condensed tree. Here we have per cluster a birth_lambda (lambda at which cluster is formed) and per point exit_lambdas (likely the lambda at which point exits the cluster, i.e., becomes part of a larger cluster or noise). So stability per cluster = sum over points of (point_exit - cluster_birth) positive part.\n\nBut note: point_exit_lambdas may contain values less than birth_lambda? Actually point_exit should be >= cluster_birth because a point can only exit after cluster birth? Not necessarily, but we take max(0, exit - birth).\n\nSo compute stability for each retained cluster (size >= min_cluster_size). Also note: some clusters may have size < min_cluster_size, we drop them entirely.\n\n4. Build hierarchy: parent dict maps cluster_id to parent_id or None for root. We need to select clusters: Rank all retained clusters by decreasing stability, then by cluster_id (string ascending? \"cluster ID\" likely lexicographic). Then select clusters greedily: iterate over sorted list, if a cluster's ancestors (including parent, grandparent, etc.) are not already selected, and if it is not blocked by a descendant (i.e., if any of its descendants have already been selected? Actually we block ancestors when we select a cluster. The rule: \"Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it, then block its ancestors.\" So if a cluster is selected, it blocks its ancestors (meaning ancestors cannot be selected later). Also, a cluster cannot be selected if any of its descendants are already selected? Actually the rule says: \"Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it\". That means if a descendant (child, grandchild) has already been selected, then this cluster is blocked (cannot be selected). So we need to check that no previously selected clust