t. Now consider cluster B that is a descendant of A. A is an ancestor of B. So B's ancestors include A. But A is in blocked set? Wait, we added ancestors of A to blocked set, not A itself. So A is not in blocked set. But we need to prevent selecting B because it has a selected descendant? Actually we need to prevent selecting B if A (a descendant of B) is already selected. But B is an ancestor of A. So when we select A, we block B (ancestor). So B would be in blocked set because we added ancestors of A. So that works. Conversely, if we consider a cluster that is an ancestor of a selected cluster, it is blocked. So the check \"candidate ID not in blocked set\" suffices, provided we add all ancestors of selected clusters to blocked set. But we also need to ensure that when we select a cluster, we don't later select its descendants? That is prevented because descendants are not ancestors; they will not be in blocked set from that selection. However, a descendant could later be selected if it has no selected descendants and not blocked. But is that allowed? The rule: \"Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it.\" So if we select a cluster that has a descendant already selected, that would violate because the already selected descendant blocks it. But we are iterating in order of decreasing stability, so we will encounter the descendant first because it has higher stability? Not necessarily, but the algorithm should handle any order. The typical condensed tree selection algorithm (as in HDBSCAN) sorts clusters by stability descending, then iterates, for each cluster, if it has no selected descendants (i.e., none of its child clusters have been selected), then select it. Then propagate to ancestors. Here we have parent links, not children links. But we can build children map from parent map. Then for a candidate cluster, we need to check if any of its descendants (by traversing children) are already selected. That could be expensive. But since cl