elect a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it.\" So blocking is only by descendants, not ancestors. So if an ancestor is already selected, is that considered? The rule doesn't say ancestors block. But in HDBSCAN, if you select a higher-level cluster, you don't select its children because they are nested. But the rule says \"descendant blocks it\". So a descendant blocks its ancestors. So if we select a cluster, it does not block its ancestors. So if we select a parent cluster first, then later we could still select a child cluster? That would violate the tree structure. So likely the rule intends that selection is bottom-up: you start with the leaves (or high stability clusters) and then block ancestors. If you select a high-level cluster first, that would be unusual because it would have many points, but its descendants (smaller clusters) would have lower stability? But the algorithm should handle any order. The typical algorithm (like in HDBSCAN's condensed tree) selects by descending stability, and for each cluster, if it has no selected children (i.e., no descendant selected), then select it. That ensures that children are not selected if ancestor is selected, because children are considered first? Actually they sort descending stability, so high stability clusters tend to be selected early. Then if a cluster with high stability is selected, its children are later considered, but they will have a selected descendant? Wait, children have the selected cluster as an ancestor, not descendant. So the condition \"no selected descendant\" for the child would be true if the child has no selected descendants (the parent is an ancestor, not descendant). So the child could be selected even though its parent is already selected. That would be wrong. So the correct condition is: a cluster can be selected only if no ancestor of it is selected, i.e., it is not nested inside a selected cluster. But the rule says \"no already selected descendant blocks it\",