er, and we added ancestors of that selected cluster, but the candidate is a descendant, not an ancestor. So candidate will not be in blocked_ancestors. So we would incorrectly select it. So we need to also check that no ancestor of the candidate is selected. Equivalent to: candidate should not be a descendant of any selected cluster. That can be enforced by also maintaining a set of selected clusters and checking ancestors of candidate. Alternatively, when we select a cluster, we could mark all descendants as blocked? But that might be too broad. The typical algorithm: when you select a cluster, you remove its descendants from consideration (they are never considered because they are not in the list? Actually they are in the list but when you iterate, you skip them because they have selected ancestor? But the algorithm doesn't need to check ancestors because it processes in order of decreasing stability, and ancestors generally have lower stability? Not necessarily. So we need to handle both directions.\n\nLet's derive from first principles with the rule:\n- A cluster can be selected only if no descendant of it has been selected. (descendant blocks this cluster)\n- After selecting, its ancestors are blocked (cannot be selected later because they would have this cluster as a descendant).\n\nThus, a cluster cannot be selected if:\n  (a) It has a selected descendant. (blocked)\n  (b) It is an ancestor of a selected cluster? That would mean a selected descendant exists, which is already covered by (a). Actually if it is an ancestor of a selected cluster, then it has a selected descendant, so (a) applies. So (a) covers both directions? Wait, if we have selected child, then parent has selected descendant, so parent is blocked. That's (a). If we have selected parent, then child has selected ancestor, but does child have a selected descendant? No, child's descendants are none selected. So child is not blocked by (a). So child would be eligible for selection according to (a)